\f1\b\fs26 \cf2 \strokec2 Key gaps in the optimized detector:
\f0\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0
\f3 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 holiday_splash_loss
\f1\b \'a0worsened (+1.24)
\f0\b0 \'a0\'97 the optimizer traded off holiday spillover accuracy to reduce holiday event/impact losses. The detector is now\'a0
\f2\i worse
\f0\i0 \'a0at capturing the decay of holiday effects into neighboring days.\
\ls2\ilvl0
\f3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 seasonality_strength_loss
\f1\b \'a0worsened (+0.22)
\f0\b0 \'a0\'97 the optimizer degraded seasonality strength estimation to improve pattern matching. The detector under/over-estimates how strong seasonality actually is.\
\ls2\ilvl0
\f3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 noise_level_loss
\f1\b \'a0is stuck at 2.0
\f0\b0 \'a0\'97 this is a significant absolute loss that did not budge at all. The detector's noise magnitude estimation is never being improved; it may indicate the noise level estimator has no tunable parameters exposed to the optimizer, or the parameter search space doesn't include the relevant knobs.\
\ls2\ilvl0
\f3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	4	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 noise_regime_loss
\f1\b \'a0is stuck at 0.36
\f0\b0 \'a0\'97 similarly immovable. Noise regime transition detection was never improved. Together with noise_level_loss, the entire noise characterization pipeline is\'a0
\f1\b completely unoptimized
\f0\b0 .\
\ls2\ilvl0
\f3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	5	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 metadata_loss
\f1\b \'a0is stuck at 0.63
\f0\b0 \'a0\'97 series scale/type classification is not optimized, suggesting these metadata detection parameters aren't part of the optimizer's search space.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\fs31\fsmilli15600 \cf2 C. Gaps in the Loss Function/Match Statistics Themselves\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\fs26 \cf2 Looking at what the loss function\'a0
\f2\i measures
\f0\i0 \'a0versus what it\'a0
\f2\i doesn't
\f0\i0 :\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 No real-data validation loss
\f0\b0 \'a0\'97 The entire loss framework only evaluates against\'a0
\f1\b synthetic ground truth
\f0\b0 . There is no term that checks whether the detector's outputs are plausible on\'a0
\f1\b real data
\f0\b0 . This is the most fundamental gap: the tuned detector could be perfectly calibrated for synthetic data yet poorly suited for reality (exactly the sim-to-real gap we see in section A).\
\ls3\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 No cross-validation or generalization metric
\f0\b0 \'a0\'97 The optimizer trains and evaluates on the\'a0
\f1\b same
\f0\b0 \'a0synthetic dataset. There is no held-out synthetic set or k-fold scheme to check for overfitting to the specific random seed.\
\ls3\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Missing distribution-level comparison
\f0\b0 \'a0\'97 The loss only measures aggregate/per-series errors but doesn't compare\'a0
\f1\b distributions
\f0\b0 \'a0of detected features vs. true features (e.g., "are detected anomaly magnitudes distributed like true magnitudes?"). This matters when counts are right but the character of detections is wrong.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0
\f3 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	4	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 seasonality_changepoint_loss
\f1\b \'a0is always 0.0
\f0\b0 \'a0\'97 in both baseline and optimized. Either the synthetic data has no seasonality changepoints (making this loss dead weight), or the detector never triggers them. Either way, this component is non-functional.\
\ls3\ilvl0
\f3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	5	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 regressor_loss
\f1\b \'a0is always 0.0
\f0\b0 \'a0\'97 same issue; if the synthetic data doesn't include external regressors, this term contributes nothing and can't be trained.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	6	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 No temporal precision metric
\f0\b0 \'a0\'97 While the anomaly loss internally uses a 1-day tolerance for matching, the reported\'a0
\f3 anomaly_loss
\f0 \'a0is a scalar aggregate. There's no\'a0
\f1\b separate
\f0\b0 \'a0metric showing the distribution of timing errors (e.g., "median detection lag is 0.5 days, 90th percentile is 2 days"). The same applies to trend changepoints and level shifts.\
\ls3\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	7	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 No false-positive rate as a standalone metric
\f0\b0 \'a0\'97 False positives are folded into the same loss as missed detections (e.g., in anomaly loss:\'a0
\f3 0.15 * count + 0.1 * sqrt(count)
\f0 ). There is no separate precision/recall/F1 breakdown, making it hard to diagnose whether the detector is biased toward over-detection or under-detection.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0
\f3 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	8	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 invalid value encountered in divide
\f1\b \'a0warnings
\f0\b0 \'a0\'97 These NumPy warnings appear repeatedly during both optimization and evaluation, suggesting division-by-zero in seasonality or noise calculations. This means some series are producing degenerate computations, and those loss values may be NaN or silently clamped, corrupting the loss signal.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\fs31\fsmilli15600 \cf2 D. Real-Data Application (Cell 23): Red Flags\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\fs26 \cf2 When applied to the 5 real series:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Every series has exactly 4 trend changepoints
\f0\b0 \'a0\'97 this suspicious uniformity suggests the changepoint detector is hitting a hard\'a0
\f3 max
\f0 \'a0cap or the spacing parameter (
\f3 changepoint_spacing: 5040
\f0 ) is forcing exactly 4 breaks across the ~5.5-year span. The real data likely has varying numbers of structural breaks.\
\ls4\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 fb_app.ES
\f0\b0 \'a0has\'a0
\f3 noise_to_signal_ratio = 1.0794
\f0 \'a0\'97 noise exceeds signal, yet only 2 anomalies are detected. For such a noisy series, the anomaly count seems suspiciously low.\
\ls4\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Seasonality strength values are very low
\f0\b0 \'a0(0.005 \'96 0.081) \'97 given the real data's median weekly seasonality strength of 0.031, these seem plausible, but the detector was trained on synthetic data with\'a0
\f2\i much weaker
\f0\i0 \'a0weekly seasonality (0.011), so it may be mis-calibrated.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
