\fs26 \cf2 \strokec2 This is the most alarming finding.\'a0
\f1\b Across two optimizer runs with only marginally different total losses (52.99 vs 51.89), the real-data outputs change dramatically:
\f0\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Trend changepoints collapsed: 4 \uc0\u8594  1 for every single series.
\f0\b0 \'a0Run 1's changepoint params (
\f3 method: basic
\f0 ,\'a0
\f3 changepoint_spacing: 5040
\f0 ) produced exactly 4 per series. Run 2 switched to\'a0
\f3 method: pelt
\f0 \'a0with\'a0
\f3 penalty: 50
\f0 ,\'a0
\f3 min_segment_length: 15
\f0 , which produces only 1. This is a\'a0
\f1\b qualitative regime change
\f0\b0 \'a0in detector behavior \'97 the optimizer found a different local minimum with completely different changepoint philosophy. Neither count is necessarily "right" without ground truth, but the dramatic swing is concerning.\
\ls6\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Level shifts vanished.
\f0\b0 \'a0Run 1 detected 2 each for AU and BR; Run 2 detects 0 across all 5 series. The\'a0
\f3 level_shift_params
\f0 \'a0changed significantly:\'a0
\f3 window_size
\f0 \'a030\uc0\u8594 14,\'a0
\f3 alpha
\f0 \'a02.5\uc0\u8594 3.5,\'a0
\f3 max_level_shifts
\f0 \'a08\uc0\u8594 3,\'a0
\f3 alignment
\f0 \'a0changed,\'a0
\f3 remove_at_shift
\f0 \'a0flipped from True\uc0\u8594 False. The higher alpha threshold makes the detector far more conservative.\
\ls6\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Noise-to-signal ratios shifted dramatically
\f0\b0 \'a0for AU (+73%), CA (+76%), ES (+65%). This suggests the\'a0
\f3 general_transformer_params
\f0 \'a0pipeline changed enough to alter the detrended/deseasonalized residual. Run 1 used SavitzkyGolay+AlignLastValue; Run 2 uses FFTFilter (cutoff=0.05). The FFT filter leaves more variance in the "noise" component.\
\ls6\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	4	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Holiday detection swings are large.
\f0\b0 \'a0CA went from 31\uc0\u8594 40 holidays, DE from 11\u8594 17. The holiday detection method changed from IQR-based to EllipticEnvelope-based, with\'a0
\f3 threshold
\f0 \'a00.9\uc0\u8594 0.8. Run 2 also enables\'a0
\f3 use_dayofmonth_holidays: True
\f0 .\
\ls6\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	5	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Seasonality strength is stable
\f0\b0 \'a0across runs (within ~1% for each series), suggesting the seasonality estimation is the most robust component.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
