\fs26 \cf2 The most significant finding from comparing the two runs is that\'a0
\f1\b the loss function is a poor proxy for real-world detection quality
\f0\b0 . Two optimizer runs with nearly identical total losses (52.99 vs 51.89 \'97 a 2% difference) produce feature detectors that behave\'a0
\f1\b qualitatively differently
\f0\b0 \'a0on real data: one finds 4 trend changepoints per series with level shifts; the other finds 1 trend changepoint and no level shifts. The optimizer is navigating a\'a0
\f1\b multimodal loss landscape
\f0\b0 \'a0with many similarly-scoring but behaviorally distinct optima, and the loss function has no regularization toward real-data plausibility or behavioral consistency. The frozen loss components (noise, metadata, regressor, seasonality changepoints) are confirmed unoptimizable across both runs, representing 3+ points of loss that can never be reduced.\
